      "completion_tokens": 1138,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**, which means that every single molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. This means the electrons in the molecule are not shared equally between the oxygen and the hydrogen atoms. The oxygen atom has a slight negative charge, while the hydrogen atoms have a slight positive charge. This polarity is what allows water to stick to itself (cohesion) and to other substances (adhesion).\n\n### 2. States of Matter\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists as a solid, liquid, and gas at temperatures commonly found on the planet:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When water freezes, the molecules lock into a rigid crystal lattice.\n*   **Liquid:** This is the state we are most familiar with. It is the only common natural substance that exists in this state over a wide range of temperatures.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Water Vapor):** When heated, water molecules move so fast they escape into the air.\n\n### 3. Unique Properties\nWater has several physical properties that make it essential for life:\n*   **The Universal Solvent:** Because of its polarity, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This allows it to transport nutrients and waste inside living organisms.\n*   **High Specific Heat:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before it gets hot, and it releases a lot of heat before it freezes. This helps regulate the temperature of the Earth and keeps bodies of water from boiling or freezing too quickly.\n*   **Surface Tension:** Water molecules at the surface stick together tightly, creating a kind of \"skin.\" This allows small insects to walk on water and helps plants pull water up from their roots.\n\n### 4. Importance to Life\n*   **Biological Necessity:** All known forms of life require water to survive. It is the medium in which biochemical reactions occur in our cells.\n*   **Earth's Composition:** About 71% of the Earth's surface is covered in water, and 60–70% of the human body is made of water."
